%T A402118 551,562,685,794,851,893,926,951,985,1115,1415,1893,1985,3326,3415,
%U A402118 3893,3985,4115,4562,5339,5893,5926,6493,6893,7226,8158,8185,9326,9493,9985
%N A402118 Left-truncatable emirpimes in base 10, stopping at two digits.
%C A402118 An emirpimes is a semiprime whose decimal reversal is a different semiprime. Successively deleting the leftmost digit must leave an emirpimes at every step down to two digits.
%C A402118 There are 87 terms. The largest and unique nine-digit term is 359785339 (G. L. Honaker, Jr.).
%C A402118 Digit reversal gives a bijection with the right-truncatable emirpimes.
%H A402118 Eric W. Weisstein, <a href="/A402118/b402118.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..87</a> (complete sequence).
%H A402118 G. L. Honaker, Jr., <a href="https://t5k.org/curios/page.php?number_id=6055657">Prime Curios!: 933587953</a>.
%H A402118 G. L. Honaker, Jr., <a href="https://t5k.org/curios/page.php?curio_id=50971">Prime Curios!: 359785339</a>.
%H A402118 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Emirpimes.html">Emirpimes</a>.
%t A402118 emirpimesQ[n_] := With[{r = IntegerReverse[n]}, n != r && PrimeOmega[n] == 2 && PrimeOmega[r] == 2];
%t A402118 levels = NestWhileList[Select[Flatten[Outer[Plus, 10 #, Range[0, 9]]], emirpimesQ] &, Select[Range[10, 99], emirpimesQ], Length[#] > 0 &];
%t A402118 Sort[IntegerReverse /@ Flatten[levels]]
%Y A402118 Cf. A097393.
%Y A402118 Cf. A402116 (right-truncatable emirpimes).
%K A402118 nonn,base,fini,full,new
%O A402118 1,1
%A A402118 _Eric W. Weisstein_, Nov 13 2026
